<description>
Stack the colored rectangles by size after aligning their upper-left corners.

Input structure
- The dominant color is background.
- Each foreground color is the visible part of one filled rectangle.
- A color's full rectangle is recovered from the bounding box of its visible pixels.

Rule
- Build one rectangle for each foreground color using that color's bounding-box height and width.
- Use the largest rectangle's height and width as the output shape.
- Paint all rectangles at the output upper-left corner from largest area to smallest area.

Key insight
- The input positions only reveal each rectangle's size; the output re-stacks the same rectangles at a common origin.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_in_order",
  "src": "foreground color rectangles s recovered from each color's visible-pixel bbox, ordered by decreasing bbox area",
  "dst": "same-origin output layers d ordered back-to-front by decreasing area",
  "match_cond": "rank(s) == rank(d)",
  "transform": "stamp a filled rectangle of s.color and s.bbox size at the upper-left corner, overwriting earlier larger layers",
  "grid_shape": "height and width of the largest recovered rectangle"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 14-16 rows x 15-16 cols, single background color
  rectangles:
    count: 3-4 distinct foreground colors, each a solid filled rectangle
    dimensions: h 1-8, w 2-10, all areas pairwise distinct
    largest: has both max height AND max width among all rects (solver asserts this)
    nesting: every smaller rect has h <= largest.h AND w <= largest.w
    placement: scattered across grid; at least 1 overlapping bbox pair, 1-2 isolated rects
    overlap_constraint: when bboxes overlap, no entire edge row/col of the occluded rect may be hidden (visible-pixel bbox must recover true dimensions)
    bg_fraction: ~60-70% of input cells are background [observed]
  output:
    shape: largest.h x largest.w
    construction: paint rects at upper-left origin, largest-area first to smallest-area last (smallest on top)
    no background in output (largest rect fills entire output)

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Pick N=3-4, choose N distinct colors + 1 bg color
    2. Build output (the stacked rectangles) — pick largest rect dims (h 5-8, w 5-10) as output size; for each subsequent rect pick strictly smaller h and w with unique area; paint at origin largest-first
    3. For each rect, choose a random position in a 14-16 x 15-16 input grid with enough room to fit
    4. Paint rects onto input in an arbitrary z-order, ensuring at least one overlapping bbox pair exists
    5. Validate overlap constraint — for each occluded rect, check that visible pixels still span its full bbox (no entire edge row/col hidden); retry placement if violated
```
